  "response": "## Verdict: request changes — not a hard block\n\nThe finding is real and the fix is cheap, but nothing in the evidence puts `tomorrowMs` on a blocking path.\n\n**The finding, verbatim** (`crime_00019`, `dst_naive_arithmetic`, \"DST-Naive Day Math\"):\n\n> \"1 day-level millisecond constant in arithmetic. A day isn't 86,400,000 ms on DST-transition days; manual ms math drifts silently.\"\n\nEvidence:\n> - `\"+ 86400000 (day) @L52\"`\n> - `\"lines: 52\"`\n> - `\"use a calendar-aware library (Luxon, Temporal, date-fns-tz) for day math\"`\n\nScores: `severity 0.6`, `confidence 0.8`, `agent_risk 0.3`, `test_gap 0.5`, `blast_radius 0` (`blast_radius_direct_importers: 0`), `effort: \"medium\"`, `fix_shape: \"use a DST-aware library; never add seconds to a wall-clock date\"`.\n\n**Why request-changes rather than block:** medium severity at 0.8 confidence, zero measured blast radius, and a bounded one-line fix. Blocking is for high-severity findings on a shipping path. But `test_gap: 0.5` means nothing pins this behaviour, so the fix should land in this PR, not a follow-up ticket.\n\n**What would flip it to a block:** if `tomorrowMs` feeds a trial/expiry or billing boundary. The same repo has `crime_00023` — `\"1× Date.now()\"` at `src/api/state.ts:18`, `\"all 1 decide a branch or comparison\"` — next to an `expired` boolean. A helper that silently returns the wrong calendar day, feeding a comparison that decides whether a subscription is dead, is a customer-facing bug, not a tidiness issue. Ask the author which one it is before merging.\n\n## The two dates per year\n\n`+ 86_400_000` is exactly correct as *absolute elapsed time*. It is wrong whenever the caller means \"the same wall-clock time tomorrow\" — and that diverges on the two local DST transitions:\n\n| Timezone | Spring forward (23-hour day) | Fall back (25-hour day) |\n|---|---|---|\n| US (`America/*`) | 2nd Sunday in March — **8 Mar 2026** | 1st Sunday in November — **1 Nov 2026** |\n| EU (`Europe/*`) | last Sunday in March — **29 Mar 2026** | last Sunday in October — **25 Oct 2026** |\n\nConcretely, starting from local midnight on the transition day:\n\n- **Spring forward:** +24h lands at **01:00 the next day** — right date, wrong hour. Breaks any \"start of tomorrow\" assumption.\n- **Fall back:** +24h lands at **23:00 the *same* day** — **the wrong calendar day entirely.** This is the one that produces visibly wrong output: `tomorrowMs` returns today.\n\nWhich pair applies depends on the host's zone, which this file does not control — and that's the compounding problem below.\n\n## The file-level context that matters more than the one finding\n\n`src/date.ts` carries four temporal findings, and the DST one is not the worst:\n\n- `crime_00001` — Temporal Recklessness, **high**: `\"8 direct uses of Date.now()/new Date()\"`, `\"1 decide a branch or comparison (lines 4)\"`, `\"a reading in a comparison changes what the code does — tests cannot pin the behaviour without controlling the clock\"`.\n- `crime_00009` — Half-UTC, Half-Local, **high**: `\"\\\"d\\\" uses getUTCFullYear() @L9 and getMonth() @L41\"`, `\"The two families differ silently by the host's UTC offset.\"`\n- `crime_00013` — Timezone Roulette, **medium**: `\"unsafe literals: \\\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\\\", \\\"2026-12-20\\\"\"`.\n\nThe file already has no consistent answer to \"which timezone am I in?\", and `tomorrowMs` inherits that ambiguity. So the review comment I'd actually leave:\n\n> Two questions, and the answer to the first decides the second. (1) Does `tomorrowMs` mean \"24 hours from now\" (absolute) or \"same time tomorrow\" (calendar)? If absolute, keep the arithmetic and rename it `plus24hMs` so the next reader — human or agent — doesn't reach for it for day math. If calendar, swap to `Temporal.ZonedDateTime.add({days: 1})` or `date-fns-tz`, per the finding's suggested action. (2) Either way, add a test that pins the fall-back date in a fixed zone, since `test_gap` is 0.5 and this file reads the wall clock in a branch at line 4.\n\nThe rename is the higher-leverage half. `agent_risk: 0.3` on a helper called `tomorrowMs` understates it — the name promises calendar semantics the body doesn't deliver, and that's precisely the mismatch an agent propagates to new call sites.",
